      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section id="pure-silicone">
        <h2>Pure Silicone Coatings for Extreme Temperatures</h2>
        <p>
          For service temperatures above 300°C — industrial furnaces, boiler fireboxes, incinerator afterburners, automotive exhaust stacks, chimney flues, and high-temperature process vessels — only pure silicone resin systems, formulated without any organic co-resin, provide adequate binder stability. These coatings use 100% polysiloxane resin as the film-forming binder, rely exclusively on heat-stable inorganic pigments and fillers, and are engineered to crosslink through the condensation of silanol groups (Si-OH) at elevated temperature — a cure mechanism that requires thermal energy to complete and cannot be bypassed by ambient-temperature drying.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cure chemistry of pure silicone coatings is critical to their performance and is frequently misunderstood in industrial practice. Freshly applied pure silicone film is not at full crosslink density — it has dried through solvent evaporation to a handleable condition but is not yet fully cured. Full cure and the rated temperature performance are only achieved after a controlled burn-in sequence: typically a staged heating programme from ambient to the maximum service temperature in incremental steps of 50–100°C, with dwell periods at each stage to allow volatile cure by-products (methanol, water) to escape without creating blisters in the film. This burn-in is specified in the product data sheet and is a non-negotiable quality step. <a href="https://www.astm.org/d2485-23.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D2485</a> defines the standard test procedures for evaluating coatings in high-temperature service and classifies coatings into temperature categories that form the basis of most product specifications.
        </p>

