      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="pigments-fillers">
        <h2>Heat-Stable Pigments and Fillers</h2>
        <p>
          The pigment selection in heat-resistant coatings is as critical as the binder chemistry. Standard organic pigments — phthalocyanines, azo compounds, quinacridones — decompose irreversibly at temperatures well below the rated service temperature of even the most basic silicone-alkyd system, causing discolouration, tinting changes, and potential film degradation from decomposition products. Every pigment and extender included in a heat-resistant formulation must be characterised and verified for thermal stability at the intended service temperature, and inorganic pigments are the only technically defensible choice for applications above 200°C.
        </p>
        <p>
          Aluminium flake and powder are the dominant pigments in high-temperature silicone coatings for three distinct reasons: aluminium reflects infrared radiation (reducing absorbed heat at the substrate surface), provides electrochemical sacrificial corrosion protection to steel, and orients as lamellar flakes parallel to the film surface during drying, creating a tortuous diffusion path that impedes moisture and oxygen ingress. Aluminium is thermally stable as a metal well above 600°C, making it compatible with the highest-performance pure silicone systems. Iron oxide red (α-Fe₂O₃, hematite) is the standard black and red pigment for moderate-temperature applications — it is thermally stable as a phase to approximately 500°C. Chromium oxide green (Cr₂O₃) is one of the most thermally stable coloured pigments available, with a melting point above 2400°C and essentially unlimited temperature resistance within any practically achievable coating service temperature. Carbon black provides deep black colour with excellent thermal stability at all practical service temperatures. Titanium dioxide (rutile grade) provides white colour and is stable to above 600°C, though it can catalyse chalking in silicone films under intense UV exposure if used at high concentration. Micaceous iron oxide (MIO) contributes both a lamellar barrier structure similar to aluminium flake and moderate corrosion resistance, making it a practical extender-pigment for industrial heat-resistant topcoats.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          When selecting pigments for a heat-resistant formulation, always verify the phase stability of every inorganic pigment at the rated service temperature — not just the decomposition temperature. Iron oxide black converts irreversibly to iron oxide red at approximately 300°C; a product specified as black will arrive on site black and turn red in first service. Phase transformation temperatures must be checked against the coating's ASTM D2485 category before any colour commitment is made.
        </div>
      </section>

