      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="application-testing">
        <h2>Application, Surface Preparation &amp; Performance Testing</h2>
        <p>
          Surface preparation is the single most critical factor governing heat-resistant coating performance in service — more important than binder selection, pigment loading, or application method. Steel substrates must be blast-cleaned to a minimum of Sa 2.5 (near-white metal) per <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/72975.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 8501-1</a> to remove all mill scale, rust, and surface contamination that would compromise adhesion at the metal-coating interface. At service temperatures above 400°C, standard primer coats are not used — any organic primer binder will thermally degrade before the topcoat does, undermining the adhesion of the entire system from below. The blast-cleaned metal profile (typically 40–70 µm Rz) provides the mechanical anchor required for direct adhesion of the silicone topcoat to metal.
        </p>
        <p>
          Application is conducted by conventional spray (airless or air-assisted airless), brush, or roller at the dry film thickness specified in the product data sheet — typically 25–40 µm per coat for standard single-coat systems. Applying above the specified film thickness is a common cause of blistering during the initial burn-in: thick films trap residual solvent and condensation cure by-products that must be able to diffuse out of the film during thermal cure. Multiple thin coats, each dried before the next is applied, are the correct approach where increased film build is required. Performance evaluation follows ASTM D2485, which defines heat resistance test methods across four temperature categories and evaluates the coating for adhesion, film integrity, discolouration, blistering, and corrosion resistance both before and after thermal exposure. Salt spray testing per ASTM B117 is typically conducted after the heat cycle to assess whether the thermal exposure has compromised the coating's corrosion barrier function.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What temperature can heat resistant paint withstand?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Temperature resistance depends on the resin system and pigment selection. Silicone-alkyd hybrids perform continuously to 200–250°C. Silicone-epoxy hybrids extend to 250–300°C. High-silicone-content systems reach 300–400°C. Pure polydimethylsiloxane coatings with aluminium pigment provide continuous protection at 500–600°C and intermittent protection at higher temperatures. Performance is tested per ASTM D2485, which classifies coatings across temperature categories from 150°C to over 500°C continuous service temperature.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between silicone-alkyd and pure silicone heat resistant paint?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Silicone-alkyd is a chemical hybrid (20–50% silicone by weight) that air-dries at ambient temperature and resists up to 200–250°C. It is more economical and easier to apply but limited by the organic alkyd co-resin component. Pure silicone uses 100% polysiloxane binder with no organic co-resin — it requires thermal burn-in to cure fully but resists 400–600°C because the Si-O-Si backbone is inherently more stable than any carbon-chain polymer. Pure silicone sacrifices application convenience for a fundamentally higher thermal ceiling.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why is aluminium powder used as a pigment in high-temperature coatings?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Aluminium flake provides three simultaneous functions: it reflects infrared radiation reducing absorbed heat at the substrate surface; it acts as a sacrificial anode providing galvanic corrosion protection to steel; and lamellar flakes orient parallel to the film surface creating a tortuous diffusion barrier against moisture and oxygen. Aluminium is thermally stable as a metal well above 600°C, making it the primary pigment choice for pure silicone systems operating at the upper limit of heat-resistant coating performance.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does heat resistant paint need a primer?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">At moderate temperatures (up to 250°C), a compatible heat-resistant primer (silicone-modified epoxy or silicone-alkyd) improves adhesion and corrosion protection. Above 400°C, conventional primers are thermally unstable and must not be used — the substrate must be blast-cleaned to Sa 2.5 per ISO 8501-1 and the silicone topcoat applied directly to the prepared metal. Some pure silicone systems are formulated as single-coat direct-to-metal products incorporating corrosion-inhibiting pigments to compensate for the absence of a primer.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can heat resistant paint cure at room temperature, or does it need oven curing?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Silicone-alkyd hybrids air-dry at ambient temperature through oxidative crosslinking, but achieve rated performance only after a thermal cure or controlled burn-in sequence. Pure silicone systems require a minimum initial cure temperature of 200–250°C to complete the condensation crosslinking of silanol groups (Si-OH). The burn-in protocol — staged heating in 50–100°C increments with dwell periods at each stage — is specified in the product data sheet and cannot be bypassed without compromising adhesion, film integrity, and rated temperature performance.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What causes heat resistant paint to peel or blister after service?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">The most common cause is inadequate surface preparation — mill scale, rust, oil, or moisture contamination on the substrate undermines adhesion at the metal interface. Applying a coating rated below the actual service temperature causes binder thermal degradation and film failure. Excess film thickness traps solvent and cure by-products during burn-in, creating internal blisters. Thermal cycling fatigue — repeated expansion and contraction between the coating and metal at different rates — causes progressive adhesion loss in aggressive service environments. Each failure mode requires a specific corrective action: re-blasting, specification upgrade, film thickness reduction, or evaluation of a more flexible silicone system respectively.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

